"Dividing by zero is undefined.": "852fffdb354e",
"Division and multiplication are partners. When the dealing is done, line up each plate's candies in a row. Three plates of four make three rows of four, which is the tray from the last lesson: 3 × 4 = 12. So 12 ÷ 3 = 4, because 3 × 4 = 12. This is also how to check an answer. Multiply the divisor by the quotient, add the remainder, and you should get back the number you started with: 3 × 4 + 1 = 13.": "53051ab1a802",
"Here are two questions about the same twelve candies. First: you share the twelve candies equally among three friends. How many does each friend get? Second: you pack the twelve candies into bags, with three candies in each bag. How many bags do you fill? Both questions use the numbers 12 and 3, and both answers are 4. But they are not the same question, and if you act them out, your hands do very different things.": "1e010fa705ba",
"Here are two questions about the same twelve candies. First: you share the twelve candies equally among three friends. How many does each friend get? Second: you pack the twelve candies into bags, with three candies in each bag. How many bags do you fill? Both questions use the numbers 12 and 3, and both answers are 4. But they are not the same question, and if you act them out, your hands do very different things. To answer the first question, you deal the candies out, one to each friend, around and around, like cards. You already know how many groups there are: three friends. You are finding out how big each group gets. American teachers often call this fair sharing. Taiwan's curriculum calls it pingfen, sharing equally. Dealing one at a time is a simple way to make sure that nobody gets more than anyone else. To answer the second question, you fill one bag with three candies, then a second bag, then a third, until you run out. This time you know how big each group is: three candies. You are finding out how many groups you can make. Even so, both questions are written the same way: 12 ÷ 3 = 4, read “twelve divided by three equals four.” The number being divided, 12, is the dividend. The number you divide by, 3, is the divisor. The answer, 4, is the quotient. Now try thirteen candies. Deal them to three friends, and each gets four, but one candy is left on the table. There is not enough for another round. The leftover is called the remainder. An American classroom writes 13 ÷ 3 = 4 R 1, where R stands for remainder. In Chinese you say the quotient is 4 and the remainder is 1. The remainder is always smaller than the divisor. If three candies were left, there would be enough for one more round. Division and multiplication are partners. When the dealing is done, line up each plate's candies in a row. Three plates of four make three rows of four, which is the tray from the last lesson: 3 × 4 = 12. So 12 ÷ 3 = 4, because 3 × 4 = 12. This is also how to check an answer. Multiply the divisor by the quotient, add the remainder, and you should get back the number you started with: 3 × 4 + 1 = 13. One number needs special care: zero. If you have no candies, everyone gets zero, so 0 ÷ 3 = 0. But you cannot divide by zero. Try sharing twelve candies among zero people, or packing them into bags that hold zero candies each. You could keep filling empty bags forever and never use up a single candy. Mathematicians say that dividing by zero is undefined: it has no answer at all.": "85e463179327",
